<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Every Layer Gets the Same Budget — But Not Every Layer Does the Same Work</h2>
<p>A transformer language model stacks <em>L</em> identical-width layers, each with the same hidden dimension <em>d</em>. The number of parameters per layer scales as <em>O(d²)</em> — quadratically — so once you choose <em>d</em>, every layer gets exactly that budget, forever, regardless of what it does.</p>
<p>This uniformity is convenient but not obviously correct. Decades of probing research shows that different layers do very different things: early layers process syntax and surface patterns, middle layers encode semantic structure and factual associations, and late layers convert that structure into next-token predictions. Why should a layer that mostly copies token surface forms get the same parameter budget as one performing rich semantic computation?</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Layer function specialization — what each region actually does</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">different roles → why uniform width is wasteful</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <svg id="specialization-svg" viewBox="0 0 640 130" style="width:100%;max-width:640px;display:block;margin:0 auto;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      <!-- Background layers row -->
      <!-- Early layers (purple) -->
      <rect x="10" y="18" width="178" height="72" rx="7" fill="rgba(139,92,246,.12)" stroke="rgba(139,92,246,.35)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="99" y="10" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#8b5cf6" font-weight="600" letter-spacing=".06em">EARLY LAYERS (1–10)</text>
      <text x="99" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#c4b5fd">Tokenization</text>
      <text x="99" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#c4b5fd">Surface patterns</text>
      <text x="99" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#c4b5fd">Positional layout</text>
      <!-- Middle layers (amber) -->
      <rect x="196" y="18" width="250" height="72" rx="7" fill="rgba(245,158,11,.1)" stroke="rgba(245,158,11,.3)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="321" y="10" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#f59e0b" font-weight="600" letter-spacing=".06em">MIDDLE LAYERS (11–30)</text>
      <text x="321" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#fcd34d">Semantic associations</text>
      <text x="321" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#fcd34d">Factual knowledge</text>
      <text x="321" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#fcd34d">Compositional structure</text>
      <!-- Late layers (green) -->
      <rect x="454" y="18" width="176" height="72" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.1)" stroke="rgba(16,185,129,.3)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="542" y="10" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#10b981" font-weight="600" letter-spacing=".06em">LATE LAYERS (31–40)</text>
      <text x="542" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#6ee7b7">Token prediction</text>
      <text x="542" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#6ee7b7">Distribution shaping</text>
      <text x="542" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="#6ee7b7">Output refinement</text>
      <!-- Uniform width bar -->
      <text x="320" y="108" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#6b7280">Uniform transformer: same width d=1600 at every layer (2B model)</text>
      <text x="320" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#8b5cf6">&gt;&lt;former: wider at purple/green regions, narrower at amber region</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
</div>

<p>MIT's &gt;&lt;former (pronounced "X-former") tests this hypothesis directly. They train decoder-only language models at four sizes (200M – 2B parameters, plus a 3B MoE) with <em>nonuniform</em> width profiles — where each layer can have a different hidden dimension — and compare against parameter-matched constant-width baselines.</p>
<p>The key finding: a <strong>×-shaped profile</strong> — wider in the early and late layers, narrower in the middle — consistently outperforms uniform-width transformers, while requiring fewer FLOPs and smaller KV cache memory. The improvement is systematic, not cherry-picked.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-valley" data-code="valley">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Compression Valley</div>
<h2>Middle Layers Collapse — Representations Become Nearly Identical</h2>
<p>Before explaining why the ×-shape works, we need to understand what goes wrong in the middle of a standard transformer. Recent analyses have discovered a systematic pathology called the <strong>compression valley</strong>: the representational diversity of the residual stream — measured by normalized matrix entropy — crashes near zero in the middle layers.</p>
<p>Normalized matrix entropy uses the singular value decomposition of the token representation matrix across a corpus. When entropy is high, the model uses its full dimensional budget to represent tokens distinctly. When entropy collapses to near zero, the representations occupy a low-rank subspace — hundreds of tokens are nearly identical vectors despite the large width.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Normalized Matrix Entropy (de Llano et al., 2026)</div>
  H(X) = <span class="math-var">−</span>(1/log r) · Σ <span class="math-var">pⱼ</span> log <span class="math-var">pⱼ</span> <span class="math-comment">   where pⱼ = σⱼ² / ‖X‖²_F</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">σⱼ = singular values of token-representation matrix X; r = rank; H∈[0,1]</span>
  <br>
  H→0: representations collapsed into a 1D subspace (all tokens ≈ same vector)
  <br>
  H→1: representations evenly distributed across all dimensions (full capacity used)
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Compression valley — normalized entropy across 40 layers (2B model)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover over chart to inspect values</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="valley-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;cursor:crosshair"></canvas>
    <div id="valley-tooltip" style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2);margin-top:.5rem;min-height:1.2rem;text-align:center"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The constant-width transformer (grey) shows a dramatic valley in layers 10–28: entropy falls to near zero, meaning the model is effectively not using most of its dimensional capacity in these layers despite having it. The &gt;&lt;former (purple) avoids this — it actively compresses in early layers (anticipating the width reduction), then maintains much higher entropy through the middle and final layers.</p>
<p>Why does the physical bottleneck help? By constraining the middle layers to actually have fewer dimensions, the model cannot "waste" capacity the way wide layers do. It is forced to use what it has efficiently. This is the &gt;&lt;former's structural regularization effect.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-shape" data-code="shape">
<div class="section-no">03 — The ×-Shape</div>
<h2>Wide Ends, Narrow Middle — and Why This Shape Wins</h2>
<p>The paper compares four shapes for nonuniform width allocation. Each shape is parameterized by two ratios: the bottleneck layer index ratio <em>r<sub>ℓ</sub></em> (where the narrowest point occurs, as a fraction of total depth) and the bottleneck dimension ratio <em>r<sub>d</sub></em> (how narrow the narrowest layer is, as a fraction of the base width d).</p>

