<p>The ×-shape consistently outperforms all alternatives on pre-training loss for the same parameter count. Surprisingly, the 3-shape (middle layers wide, ends narrow) is the <em>worst</em> performer — the opposite of what intuition might suggest. The paper shows that the optimal configuration is <em>r<sub>ℓ</sub> = 0.75L</em> (bottleneck at 75% depth, not at the midpoint) and <em>r<sub>d</sub> = 0.3</em> (bottleneck width = 30% of base width d).</p>
<p>For the 2B model (40 layers, d=1600): bottleneck lands at <strong>layer 30</strong>, with dimension <strong>d=480</strong>. The geometry is asymmetric — the first 29 layers gradually narrow from 1600 to 480, while the final 10 layers rapidly widen back to 1600.</p>

