          <div class="step-desc">When expanding, each coordinate i is restored from the <em>most recent layer</em> that actually processed dimension i. For dims [480:960], that's the wide layer at step 1 — those values are carried forward in the residual. No projection matrix, no extra parameters.</div>
          <div class="step-code"># For each dim i in [0:d_new]:
#   ℓ' = last layer where d_ℓ' ≥ i
new_input[i] = residual_at_layer[ℓ'][i]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Why carry-forward beats projection</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Ablations at 500M scale show: carry-forward (loss 3.099) vs. zero-padding (3.124) vs. a learned projection matrix (3.150). The projection adds parameters and changes the skip path — it <em>hurts</em> more than a parameter-free copy. Zero-padding also underperforms because it erases the bypassed information.</div>
          <div class="step-code">Carry-forward: loss 3.099  ← best
Zero-pad:      loss 3.124
Projection:    loss 3.150  ← worst</div>
        </div>
