<p>The mathematical interpretation is elegant: the variable-width model is equivalent to a <em>uniform-width</em> model with width equal to the widest layer, except each block only reads/writes a subset of residual stream dimensions. The narrower blocks effectively have a restricted receptive field in embedding space — a form of architectural regularization without any additional parameters.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-efficiency" data-code="efficiency">
<div class="section-no">05 — Why It's Cheaper</div>
<h2>Parameter-Matching Guarantees Lower FLOPs and Smaller KV Cache</h2>
<p>The efficiency argument is mathematically clean and relies on a single inequality: <strong>the square of the mean is always ≤ the mean of the squares</strong> (Jensen's inequality). When you match the parameter count of a variable-width model to a constant-width baseline, you are equating the sum of squared layer dimensions. But this forces the average layer dimension to be strictly smaller than the baseline d.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Why average layer width must be smaller (parameter-matched)</div>
  Params ∝ Σ <span class="math-var">dₗ²</span> = L·<span class="math-var">d²</span>  (matching constant-width baseline)
  <br><br>
  Jensen's inequality: (mean dₗ)² ≤ mean dₗ²
  <br>
  ∴  (1/L · Σdₗ)² ≤ 1/L · Σdₗ²  =  <span class="math-var">d²</span>
  <br>
  ∴  <strong>avg(dₗ) &lt; d</strong>   when widths are non-uniform
  <br><br>
  <span class="math-comment">KV cache size ∝ avg(dₗ)  →  guaranteed smaller</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">Attention FLOPs ∝ N²·Σdₗ = N²·L·avg(dₗ)  →  guaranteed fewer</span>
</div>

<p>Because transformer layer parameters scale as <em>O(d²)</em> but attention FLOPs scale as <em>O(N²·d)</em>, matching parameter count with a ×-shaped profile necessarily reduces the average layer width — and hence reduces both attention FLOPs and KV cache footprint simultaneously. The dense (matrix multiplication) FLOPs stay equal because those scale as O(d²) per token. Only the attention dot-product FLOPs are reduced.</p>
<p>Under matched-loss scaling curves (fitting loss vs. FLOPs), the gap widens: &gt;&lt;former can reach the 2B constant-width model's loss with only <strong>77.8% of pre-training FLOPs</strong> and <strong>85.1% of average KV cache width</strong>. Both the intercept (better starting point) and the scaling exponent (steeper improvement with scale) favor &gt;&lt;former.</p>

<div class="stat-row">
  <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-val purple">77.8%</div><div class="stat-lbl">FLOPs needed to match<br>2B baseline loss (scaling law)</div></div>
  <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-val cyan">85.1%</div><div class="stat-lbl">KV cache width to match<br>2B baseline loss (scaling law)</div></div>
  <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-val green">10–11%</div><div class="stat-lbl">direct KV cache reduction<br>at same parameter count</div></div>
</div>
</section>

