<p>The paper frames this as uncovering a "previously unnoticed degree of freedom in transformer design." The uniform-width assumption has been so deeply embedded in transformer infrastructure that no one had systematically tested whether it was optimal. It isn't. A simple structural change — making the middle thinner — systematically frees capacity, improves representations, and reduces compute without any changes to training procedure, data, or optimizer.</p>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display:none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Variable-Width Transformers — The &gt;&lt;Former</h2>
    <p>MIT's &gt;&lt;former challenges the standard assumption that every transformer layer should have the same hidden dimension. By giving early and late layers wider representations while making middle layers narrower — a shape that looks like an hourglass or the letter X — the model consistently outperforms parameter-matched constant-width baselines across sizes from 200M to 2B parameters, plus a 3B mixture-of-experts model.</p>
    <p>The core insight is that middle layers in standard transformers routinely collapse their representations. Measured by normalized matrix entropy — how evenly the model uses its dimensional budget — standard transformers drop to near-zero entropy in the middle layers. They possess the capacity but fail to use it. The &gt;&lt;former physically constrains middle layers to be narrower, forcing efficient use of whatever capacity they have.</p>
    <p>The fixed-residual construction makes this work without extra parameters: a single global residual stream runs at the width of the widest layer, and each block reads and writes only its own slice. Unused dimensions bypass narrow blocks unchanged and are carried forward for later wide layers to restore. Ablations show this carry-forward approach outperforms both zero-padding and learned projection matrices.</p>
    <p>Parameter-matching guarantees efficiency gains by Jensen's inequality: when squared layer dimensions sum to the same total, the average dimension must be strictly smaller for a variable-width model — automatically reducing KV cache size by 10 to 11 percent and attention FLOPs. Under matched-loss scaling curves, &gt;&lt;former achieves the 2B baseline's quality with only 77.8 percent of pre-training FLOPs.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：可变宽度Transformer——&gt;&lt;former</h2>
    <p>MIT的&gt;&lt;former架构挑战了Transformer每一层都应具有相同隐藏维度的标准假设。通过让早期层和晚期层具有更宽的表示，而使中间层更窄——形成类似沙漏或字母X的形状——该模型在200M到2B参数的所有规模上，以及3B的混合专家模型上，均持续优于等参数的等宽基线模型。</p>
    <p>核心洞察在于：标准Transformer的中间层通常会出现表示坍缩现象。用归一化矩阵熵来衡量模型对其维度预算的利用程度，标准Transformer在中间层的熵接近于零。这些层拥有容量，却没有有效使用。&gt;&lt;former通过物理约束使中间层更窄，迫使其高效利用现有容量。</p>
    <p>固定残差结构使这一切无需额外参数即可实现：一条全局残差流以最宽层的宽度运行，每个块只读写其对应的维度切片。未使用的维度绕过较窄的块保持不变，并被向前传递供后续较宽层恢复使用。消融实验表明，这种前向传递方法优于零填充和可学习投影矩阵。</p>
    <p>参数匹配通过Jensen不等式保证了效率提升：当各层维度的平方和相等时，可变宽度模型的平均维度必然严格更小——自动将KV缓存大小减少10至11个百分点，并减少注意力运算量。在等损失缩放曲线下，&gt;&lt;former以仅77.8%的预训练计算量即可达到2B基线模型的质量。</p>
  </div>
</div>

